<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors in Our World – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Purple Flower.</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify the topic of a conversation that involves familiar subjects. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of age-appropriate numbers when paired with familiar objects (e.g., identifying "three blue flowers" by pointing to the correct group). Can identify colors and objects when spoken about (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower"). Can follow simple instructions related to colors and familiar objects by pointing or gesturing (e.g., "Find the blue bird.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge about colors, and introduce core vocabulary and pronunciation in a fun, interactive way, primarily through listening and TPR.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" that incorporates colors (e.g., "Hello, hello, what color do you see? Red, red, red for you and me!"). The teacher then displays large, colorful flashcards of basic colors (red, blue, green, orange, purple) and realia (a red ball, a green leaf, a blue toy bird). Students point to the colors as the teacher names them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, for example, a blue bird. "Look! It's a bird. What color is it? It's blue. A blue bird." The teacher clearly articulates "blue bird," emphasizing the /l/ sound in "blue." The teacher then shows a leaf and says, "Look! A leaf. It's green." The teacher models the initial sound /l/ in "leaf." Students repeat chorally. The teacher demonstrates pointing to the correct object when a color and noun are named. "Point to the blue bird."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions, encouraging pointing or nodding.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a red flower flashcard) "Is this blue?" (Students shake heads or say "No.") "What color is it?" (Students point to red or say "Red.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a green leaf) "Is this a house?" (Students shake heads or say "No.") "What is it?" (Students point to the leaf or say "Leaf.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (Day/Month/Year)</p><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage provides new language input, focusing on listening for key vocabulary (colors, objects) within a simple narrative or dialogue related to the theme "That's a Purple Flower."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher uses a simple audio story or a teacher-read dialogue accompanied by large, colorful picture cards or a simple visual aid (e.g., a poster of a garden scene with different colored objects: `bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky`). The teacher narrates slowly and clearly:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look at the sky! The sky is blue." (Teacher points to the sky in the picture.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"See the tree? The tree is green. A green tree."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Oh, look! A little cat. It is an orange cat. An orange cat."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"And here... a flower! What color is it? That's a purple flower. A purple flower." (Emphasize the theme.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"See the red bird? A red bird is flying near the house."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated story/dialogue (picture book or large poster) or a teacher-created audio recording with distinct voices, clear pronunciation, and repetition of target vocabulary (color + noun).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen and respond by pointing.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> After the initial listening, "What do you see?" (Students point to objects in the picture). "What colors do you hear?" (Students point to color flashcards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> The teacher plays/reads the story again. "Point to the blue sky." "Point to the orange cat." "Point to the purple flower." "Point to the red bird."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (Day/Month/Year)</p><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled listening practice, ensuring students can accurately identify and discriminate between target vocabulary (colors and objects) when spoken.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Color + noun (e.g., "blue bird"), Present simple (e.g., "It is an orange cat."). Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky, blue, purple, orange, green, red. Pragmatic: Identifying familiar objects by color.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Touch the Color/Object."</b> The teacher spreads out a variety of large, colored flashcards (red, blue, green, orange, purple) and object flashcards (bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky) on the floor or whiteboard. The teacher calls out a single word (e.g., "Blue!", "Bird!", "Flower!") and students quickly listen and touch the corresponding card. Then, the teacher calls out "color + noun" combinations (e.g., "Blue bird!", "Red flower!", "Green tree!"). Students listen and touch the correct combination or the appropriate individual cards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Listen and Circle/Point."</b> Students are given a worksheet with several groups of objects (e.g., three flowers: one red, one blue, one purple; two birds: one green, one orange; four leaves: green, red, yellow, brown). The teacher gives instructions: "Circle the red flower." "Point to the green bird." "Circle the orange cat." The teacher repeats each instruction twice slowly. This allows students to listen for the color and the object and make a choice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Listening: "Find My Color-Object."</b> In small groups or pairs, students have a set of colored object flashcards face up. The teacher calls out a description, for example, "Find the green leaf!" or "Find the orange cat!" The groups/pairs listen and one student from each group points to the correct card. The teacher can then ask, "What is it?" and the students, if able, can echo "Green leaf" or "Orange cat." This activity reinforces both recognition and initial verbalization if possible for 1st grade, but the primary focus remains listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (Day/Month/Year)</p><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">Time: 15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In this stage, students apply their listening skills to complete a communicative task related to the "Colors in Our World" scenario, demonstrating comprehension of color and object descriptions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "My Colorful World Scene."</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a large piece of paper (representing a blank landscape) and pre-cut shapes of objects (trees, flowers, birds, houses, cats, leaves) in various colors. The teacher gives a series of instructions, guiding students to create a scene. This is a Total Physical Response (TPR) activity where listening is paramount.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, take a green tree. Put the green tree on your paper."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Now, find a blue bird. Put the blue bird near the tree."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Oh, look! A purple flower! Take a purple flower. Put the purple flower here." (Teacher points to a specific area on their own example paper). (Connects to Theme: "That's a Purple Flower.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find a red house. Place the red house on the paper."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Now, count two orange cats. One, two. Put the two orange cats by the house."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This task requires students to listen to `Color + noun` combinations and simple quantity (two) and follow multi-step instructions, resulting in a unique "Colorful World Scene" for each student.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (Day/Month/Year)</p><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage formatively assesses students' listening comprehension of colors and objects, and provides an opportunity for self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students during the "My Colorful World Scene" activity, noting their ability to correctly identify and place objects based on verbal instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Identify."</b> The teacher calls out a color and an object (e.g., "Show me the green tree!" or "Point to the purple flower!") related to the students' completed scenes. Students individually point to the correct items in their own creations. The teacher can quickly walk around and check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in pointing to the correct object and color combination (e.g., pointing to the `purple flower` when asked).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow simple instructions involving `color + noun`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Receptive identification of numbers (e.g., "two orange cats").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked two simple questions:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you understand the color words today?" (Thumbs up / Thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you find a red object in our classroom?" (Students point).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (Day/Month/Year)</p><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">Time: 5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage allows for reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future lessons within the unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite color you heard today?" (Students can point to a color or a colored object).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you listen and show me a flower?" (Students point to a flower in their scene or a flashcard).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified colors and familiar objects when spoken about, and followed simple instructions related to colors and objects by pointing. The TPR activities were highly effective for engagement and comprehension at this grade level. Some students needed more repetition for the quantifiers "two" and "many." For the next lesson, I will ensure more visual cues and repetition for numbers when paired with objects. I will also incorporate more opportunities to hear the target vocabulary in slightly varied contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This listening lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build upon this by presenting these same vocabulary words in a simple illustrated text, allowing students to recognize the words visually and connect them to the sounds they heard today, fostering early literacy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (Day/Month/Year)</p><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">Time: 5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>